{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to NORD VPN for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3740,
    "dDurationMs": 5319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does the strong nuclear force, the fundamental\nsymmetries of nature, and a laundry detergent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9059,
    "dDurationMs": 6870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have in common? Well, they’re all important parts\nof the tale of the axion - a tale which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15929,
    "dDurationMs": 5791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may take us beyond the standard model and\nsolve one of the most vexing mysteries in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21720,
    "dDurationMs": 860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astrophysics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27800,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The history of the axion is a classic physics\ntale: intrepid scientists delve deep into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33140,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trackless mathematics in search of answers\nto a mystery. And there, against all expectations,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38970,
    "dDurationMs": 5621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they find the hint of a completely new and\nunexpected denizen of the natural world. In"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44591,
    "dDurationMs": 6509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this case the mystery was a subtle inconsistency\nin the behavior of the fundamental forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51100,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the unexpected discovery? A brand new\nparticle - the axion - which, while not yet proven"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57690,
    "dDurationMs": 7549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to exist, may explain a much more famous conundrum. The axion may explain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65239,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand the origins of the axion we\nneed to go back and look at one of the most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69369,
    "dDurationMs": 6841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "powerful concepts in physics: symmetry. We\nexpect the laws of physics to be symmetric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76210,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with respect to certain properties of the\nuniverse. If the equations describing a given"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81240,
    "dDurationMs": 6869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical process do not change when you transform\na particular property of the universe, we say that process"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88109,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is symmetric to that transformation. For example,\nmost of physics is symmetric to mirror reflections"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95289,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the world mostly works the same when you\nflip the sign of the x, y, and z axes. Another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101749,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example is flipping the charges of particles\n- positive to negative and vice versa - most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106979,
    "dDurationMs": 5611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the equations of physics hold under that\nflip. But not all. In a previous episode we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112590,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talked about how the weak nuclear force is\nNOT symmetric under combined charge and mirror"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118920,
    "dDurationMs": 7430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inversion and - or in physics-speak, the weak\nforce is not CP symmetric - its behavior changes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126350,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you flip charges and do a mirror or parity\nreflection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131320,
    "dDurationMs": 7900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, so what has all this got to do with\naxions? Well hold on, we’ll get there. Given"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139220,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CP violation in the weak force it’s natural\nto ask if it happens in the strong nuclear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145560,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force also. The strong force is the fundamental\nforce that binds quarks together into protons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151260,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and neutrons, and is mediated by the gluon\nparticle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155010,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our best theoretical description of the strong\nforce is quantum chromodynamics - QCD. That’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161680,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a deep and rich subject that will get its\nown episode before long. For now it’s enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167270,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to know that the equations of motion of the\nstrong force, derived with QCD, actually allow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173940,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "violation of CP symmetry. In fact they almost\ndemand it - and yet no such violation has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181320,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ever been observed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182480,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s one example - if the strong\nforce is CP violating, it’s predicted that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188150,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the neutron should exhibit an electric field\nlike you’d get from a pair of positive and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193370,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative charges - an electric dipole field.\nOur very sensitive measurements have found"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198660,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that no such field exists- or if it is there\nthen it’s a trillion times weaker"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203750,
    "dDurationMs": 7270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than predicted by a CP-violating QCD. This\ndiscrepancy between theory and experiment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211020,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is known as the strong CP problem and is currently\nunsolved."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215900,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the a proposed solutions to the strong CP problem is going to give us the axion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222350,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we can see how that happens, we\nneed to understand why quantum chromodynamics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227530,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predicts a CP violation in the first place.\nCompared to quantum electrodynamics, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233330,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describes electromagnetism, QCD is complicated\nto say the least."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239370,
    "dDurationMs": 7490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, the vacuum in QCD is full of\nweird structure. You might ask how can a vacuum,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246860,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aka “nothing” have structure? Well, in\nquantum field theories, the vacuum isn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250870,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really nothing. “Vacuum” is the word we\nuse to describe the lowest energy state of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255940,
    "dDurationMs": 4799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a field - which is what you’ll find when\nthere are no actual particles around, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260739,
    "dDurationMs": 7351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as we saw in previous episodes, the vacuum is a very\nlively place! Particularly in QCD, where there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268090,
    "dDurationMs": 5639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isn’t just one lowest energy state - there\nare infinite lowest energy states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273729,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the vacuum can hop between these different\nstates. But because they're all the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278349,
    "dDurationMs": 7051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy, quantum weirdness allows the QCD vacuum\nto sort of simultaneously occupy all of those states"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285400,
    "dDurationMs": 6980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at once. This bizarre structure for the vacuum\nalters the equations of motion that come from QCD,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292380,
    "dDurationMs": 8020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new terms get added and the strength of those terms is governed by a new fundamental constant - theta. It’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300400,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tricky to describe what theta actually signifies\n- in fact there are different physical interpretations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305550,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but one way to describe it is that it’s\na phase offset picked up by the quantum field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311550,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as it moves between the different possible\nminimum energy states of the vacuum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316020,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it’s these new terms - the ones added\nby the weird vacuum - that appear to violate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321360,
    "dDurationMs": 8490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CP symmetry. That means the strong force should\nshow CP violation. So why doesn’t it? One"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329850,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible explanation is that this theta value\n- the constant in front of the CP violating terms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335680,
    "dDurationMs": 6870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- is just equal to zero. That would cause\nthose terms to vanish. But there’s no good"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342550,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reason why theta should be zero - at least\nnot within the standard model of particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347729,
    "dDurationMs": 6231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics. This fundamental constant may have\nended up very close to zero just by chance,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353960,
    "dDurationMs": 5099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but physicists hate using random chance to\nexplain the precise refinement of a value"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359059,
    "dDurationMs": 8830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- what they call fine tuning. In 1977 Roberto\nPeccei and Helen Quinn proposed another solution:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367889,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what if theta isn’t a constant, but can\nchange in value, both over space and over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374099,
    "dDurationMs": 7091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time. In other words, make theta a new type\nof field - a dynamic field rather than a fundamental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381190,
    "dDurationMs": 5599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant. Theta will then naturally fall to\nzero - because that reduces the overall energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386789,
    "dDurationMs": 5511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the vacuum, and the universe always seeks\nthe lowest energy configuration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392300,
    "dDurationMs": 4509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, there is actually another solution\nto the strong CP problem its's that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396809,
    "dDurationMs": 5801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if any of the quarks are massless, CP\nsymmetry is automatically conserved. However,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402610,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as far as we can tell, none of the quarks\nare massless and so this solution is not generally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407930,
    "dDurationMs": 6239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accepted - turning theta into a quantum field\nis the most promising solution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414169,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you might recall that in quantum field\ntheory a particle is just an oscillation in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418729,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a quantum field. So with a new field - this\ntheta field - we have the potential for new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424749,
    "dDurationMs": 6621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles. Theta can oscillate very slightly\naround its value of zero - and that oscillation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431370,
    "dDurationMs": 5419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gives us the axion. It was actually that of\ntwo titans of physics - Frank Wilczek and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436789,
    "dDurationMs": 5141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steven Weinberg - who first realised that\nthis new field could be quantised to give"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441930,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rise to a new particle. Wilczeck once explained how they chose the name for their new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446970,
    "dDurationMs": 7949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle. They named it after axion detergent\nbecause it seemed to ‘clean up’ the CP problem"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454919,
    "dDurationMs": 6890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quite well. This hypothetical axion particle\nwould have no electric charge, no quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461809,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin, be extreme ly light - a tiny fraction\nof the mass of the already tiny electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467180,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would interact very weakly via the strong\nand weak nuclear forces, and via gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473330,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how can we expect to detect such an elusive particles? Well, even though axions have no electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478830,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge, they can still interact with the electromagnetic\nfield and produce photons via the strong force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485270,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They would do this by generating pairs of\nvirtual quarks which then decay into photons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491030,
    "dDurationMs": 6749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the so-called Primakoff effect. This would\nlook like an axion turning into a photon - typically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497779,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the presence of a strong magnetic field.\nAnd photons can turn into axions in a similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502770,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503770,
    "dDurationMs": 5799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this actually gives us an experiment. It should be possible to shine a light through a solid,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509569,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opaque wall. It goes like this: a light is\npassed through a strong magnetic field and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515039,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then blocked by a metal wall. But some photons\nget converted to axions in the field, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519969,
    "dDurationMs": 5701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so pass directly through the wall before turning\ninto photons again, where they can be detected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525670,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least in theory - so far several experiments\nhave not confirmed axions this way, at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532370,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so far."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533990,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One issue may be that we just can’t make\nsufficiently strong artificial magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538970,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why not let nature do at least half of\nthe heavy lifting? Well that's precisely what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544300,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the CERN Axion Solar Telescope (CAST) in Switzerland\ndoes. If axions exist then they should be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551180,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produced in reasonable quantities in the core\nof the sun. There, X-rays are constantly bouncing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557060,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off electrons and protons in the presence\nof strong electromagnetic fields. Perfect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561380,
    "dDurationMs": 7399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conditions for producing axions, among other\nthings. So the Sun’s core may spew out countless"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568779,
    "dDurationMs": 5911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "axions. CAST forms the detector part of the\napparatus and uses strong magnetic fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574690,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its own to try to turn those axions back\ninto detectable photons. No luck yet, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581160,
    "dDurationMs": 4349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the range of possible properties of axions\nis being narrowed down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585509,
    "dDurationMs": 6491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are other spacey tests for axions. Magnetars\n- highly magnetic pulsars - and quasars may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592000,
    "dDurationMs": 5899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "convert some of their own gamma ray output\ninto axions - and that dip in gamma ray output"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597899,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may be measurable. In a separate phenomenon,\nthere does appear to be a slight overabundance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603769,
    "dDurationMs": 5561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gamma rays from very distant astrophysical\nsources like blazars. A lot of those gamma"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609330,
    "dDurationMs": 5809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rays should be absorbed traveling through\nthe vast, not-quite-empty tracks of intergalactic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615139,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space on their way to us. It appears that\ntoo few of those gamma rays are absorbed, and so some astrophysicists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622269,
    "dDurationMs": 4771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have hypothesized that some gamma rays get\nconverted back and forth between axions and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627040,
    "dDurationMs": 6219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons by the magnetic fields of entire galaxies.\nThat makes them invisible for part of their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633259,
    "dDurationMs": 3331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "journey, so less likely to be blocked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636590,
    "dDurationMs": 5749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Experiments thus far have not given a reliable\npositive results, but it may be that axions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642339,
    "dDurationMs": 5791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are just lighter or more weakly interacting\nthan we think and so not detectable by current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648130,
    "dDurationMs": 5189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiments. But new experiments and upgrades\nof existing experiments will whittle away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653319,
    "dDurationMs": 6131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the parameter space of possible axion properties\n- and eventually we’ll either spot it or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659450,
    "dDurationMs": 3749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decide it’s a lost cause."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663199,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This all seems like a lot of work for a hypothetical\nparticle predicted from speculative math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668649,
    "dDurationMs": 7581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a good reason for the effort:\naxions may be ... dark matter. They have all the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676230,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right properties - no direction interaction\nwith light, and only weak interactions via"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681550,
    "dDurationMs": 5849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other forces. And although these particles\nare extremely light, axions, if they exist,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687399,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are likely to have been produced in prodigious\nnumbers in the Big Bang. That means there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692390,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could be enough of them to explain the invisible\nsource of gravity that seems to dominate the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697810,
    "dDurationMs": 6690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe - what we call dark matter. If axions\ndo turn out to be real they may clean up two of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704500,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most vexing problems in modern physics\n- the strong CP problem AND the nature of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710010,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter. Not bad for one of the tiniest\nand most elusive potential particles in all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715529,
    "dDurationMs": 1421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716950,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank NORD VPN for supporting\nPBS. Imagine VPN as a secure, encrypted tunnel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723360,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for online traffic to flow. Nobody can see\nthrough the tunnel and get their hands on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727720,
    "dDurationMs": 5429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your internet data. NordVPN allows you to\nuse public Wi-Fi in coffee shops or hotels,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733149,
    "dDurationMs": 3821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or anywhere else that you’re on someone\nelse’s network. That way you can access"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736970,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "personal and work accounts on the road, or\nsimply keep your browsing history to yourself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741790,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They don’t log your activity while you’re\nonline, they’ve got thousands of servers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746110,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 60 countries, and 24/7 customer support.\nTo learn more about NordVPN, just check out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752020,
    "dDurationMs": 1879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753899,
    "dDurationMs": 3511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we tried to figure out whether a\nuniverse that is infinite in size should contain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757410,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite repetitions of everything it contains,\nincluding you. Infinite comments ensued, of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764050,
    "dDurationMs": 3029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which I'll now answer a finite number."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767079,
    "dDurationMs": 6171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Revolver Ocelot pointed out a Medium article\nby Ethan Seigel that claimed that even if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773250,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation proceded for the entire age of the\nuniverse, the universe would still not be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778490,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "large enough to get duplicate regions. I want\nto thank you for giving me the opportunite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784190,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to pick a fight with Ethan Seigel, who is\nalmost always right. Almost. Ethan calculates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791250,
    "dDurationMs": 7550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that if the universe was inflating for its entire current age, than after those 13.5 billion years it would be around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798800,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10^10^50 times the size of our observable universe. And that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804180,
    "dDurationMs": 5219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sounds about right. He then compares that\nto an estimate of the number of possible configurations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809399,
    "dDurationMs": 9811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of particles in the universe - 10^90 factorial.\nNow, I assume Ethan got that number by imagining swapping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819210,
    "dDurationMs": 6069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around every particle in the universe in all\npossible combinations. Well, many of those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825279,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles are indistinguishable from each\nother - swapping two electrons or two photons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830129,
    "dDurationMs": 7161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn't change anything, so that number might be an over estimate. But at any rate, 10^90 factorial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837290,
    "dDurationMs": 8260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is around 10^10^90 - within a few orders of\nmagnitude. And I actually gave a larger number - the universe's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845550,
    "dDurationMs": 9010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bekenstein bound - which is around 10^124\n- giving something like 10^10^123 configurations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854560,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At any rate, it would therefore take way longer than the age of the universe for inflation to make"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860160,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a volume 10^10^90 or 123 times our obserable universe. So Eathan is right there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But inflation would get you there eventually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872440,
    "dDurationMs": 6639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as it never ended - and that's pretty\nmuch the definition of eternal inflation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879079,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ethan also points out that eternal inflation\nmust have had a beginning, so it hasn't yet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884819,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lasted for infinite time - well also true, but\nthere's no reason to imagine we are near that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891699,
    "dDurationMs": 7921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beginning, and actually 13.5 billion years would be pretty close the beginning of an infinitely-long-lasting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899620,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eternal inflation. Of course all of this is assuming that the universe is spatially finite. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907620,
    "dDurationMs": 4639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simplest interpetation of the cosmological\nequations derived from general relativity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912259,
    "dDurationMs": 7971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tell us that it it may be truly spatially infinite - in which case 10^10^90 is a piece of cake."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920230,
    "dDurationMs": 8849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paul Gaither asks how far away are these duplicate\nuniverses. Very, very far away. Roughly speaking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929079,
    "dDurationMs": 6221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you'd need to travel all the way across a\ngreater universe that is itself large enough to fit all possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935300,
    "dDurationMs": 13800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "configurations of observable universe sized regions. The\nvolume would want to be 10^10^123 (or 90) times our universe's volume,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949100,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the distance is the square root of that.\nLet's say 10^10^60-ish times the diameter of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956160,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe until things start to \nrepeat again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960290,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In last week's comment responses I corrected\nmy pronundiation for Newton's book - and then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966610,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lots of people corrected my correction. Principia\nMathematica, Prinkipia mathematica, princhipia"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972600,
    "dDurationMs": 8500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematica - many people swearing by each\nof these pronunciations. So from now on it's \"that thing Newton"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981100,
    "dDurationMs": 7310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wrote\". Anyway, it seems like some of you\nare, let's say... quite detail-oriented. And so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988410,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I was terrified you'd be mad that the infinite\ntypewriter monkey we showed was actually a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994510,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chimpanzee - so, a great ape ... totally different\nthing to a monkey. But no one even commented"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999850,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on it. C'mon guys, if we're gonna nerd, why\nnot nerd all the way?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
